// Title: ClickFix/Deno Campaign - Winget-Installed Deno Executing Remote JavaScript
// Description: Detects the core abuse pattern of the June 2026 Sophos-tracked
//   ClickFix campaign: winget.exe silently installing the Deno JavaScript
//   runtime (a legitimate, code-signed binary unlikely to be flagged by
//   reputation-based controls), followed by deno.exe fetching and running a
//   remote script directly (e.g. "deno run https://...") instead of a local
//   file. Deno's ability to execute remote code with broad permissions from
//   a user-writable directory makes this a proxy-execution technique
//   functionally similar to abusing mshta/certutil, but on a newer, less
//   monitored binary.
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1127 (Trusted Developer Utilities Proxy Execution), T1105
//   (Ingress Tool Transfer), T1059.007 (JavaScript)
// Data source: Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (DeviceProcessEvents)
// False positives: Legitimate developer workstations installing and using
//   Deno for real projects. Baseline against known dev machines / expected
//   winget deployment channels (e.g. Intune, SCCM) before escalating -
//   installs initiated by explorer.exe/PowerShell rather than a management
//   agent are the higher-confidence signal.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FileName =~ "winget.exe" and ProcessCommandLine has "deno")
    or (FileName =~ "deno.exe" and ProcessCommandLine has "run" and ProcessCommandLine has_any ("http://", "https://"))
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, FileName, FolderPath, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by Timestamp desc
